    "created_at": "2022-01-27T09:18:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-01-27T09:19:59Z",
    "body": "Ok, we had a look through the lists above and this seems potentially feasible (almost all specs we checked do the \"allowed to use\" check as their very first actionable step). \r\n\r\nThere are some cases we should discuss, in particular, a few APIs do \"fully active\" checks **before** \"allowed to use\" check (e.g., [Fullscreen API](https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#ref-for-allowed-to-use), and [WebShare](https://w3c.github.io/web-share/#share-method)). This might be a larger platform wide problem, with a lot of APIs not being \"not fully active\" aware. \r\n\r\nThe other question is: what exception should be thrown? SecurityError seems pretty popular, but maybe that can be configurable? (tho that's it's a bit ugly). \r\n\r\nThe last interesting one is for booleans operations... can it be safe to assume that \"not allowed\" would always return false... There is only the one case we have for Web Share. \r\n\r\nThe other small thing we noticed is that some editors are confusing getting \"permission to use X\" (Permissions API) with Permissions Policy (i.e., \"allowed to use\")... this might help there too. \r\n\r\n \r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
